<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basilica of San Nicola</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1087">1087</a>–1197</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Basilica of Saint Nicholas, Bari</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Basilica of San Nicola is a church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, built from 1087 to house the relics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-nicholas">Saint Nicholas</a>, which sailors from Bari had taken from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myra">Myra</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> that year. Consecrated in 1197, it is a model of Apulian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanesque-architecture">Romanesque architecture</a>. It remains a major place of pilgrimage for both Catholic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Christians.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basilica-of-san-nicola.jpg" alt="Basilica of San Nicola" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Holger Uwe Schmitt · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%22Die_Basilika_San_Nicola_ist_auch_heute_noch_ein_bedeutendes_Pilgerziel%22_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
